{"id": "5f46a495-ffca-49bb-b03f-96e6b21aff6e", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T10:48:12.223768", "source": "agent", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Program.fs looks good. Now I realize the approach of manually implementing the evaluator is fragile. Let me rethink the architecture. The proper approach is to use FsLexYacc to generate the parser, and have the parser do the evaluation directly. Let me redesign this properly.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "The current approach has a problem: I'm trying to manually evaluate tokens in Program.fs, but the real power of FsLexYacc is that the parser (Parser.fsy) generates a Parser.parse function that takes a lexer and returns the result. The parser actions in the .fsy file can directly compute the result.\n\nThe proper approach:\n1. Lexer.fsl generates Lexer.fs - a module with a `tokenize` function\n2. Parser.fsy generates Parser.fs - a module with a `parse` function that takes a lexer and returns the result\n3. Program.fs just calls Parser.parse with the lexer\n\nThe parser actions in Parser.fsy should directly compute the integer result. The grammar I wrote already does this - each production returns an int.\n\nLet me fix the approach:\n- The .fsy file generates Parser.fs with a `parse` function\n- The .fsl file generates Lexer.fs with a `tokenize` function  \n- Program.fs calls Parser.parse with the lexer buffer\n\nI need to update Program.fs to use the generated parser properly. The generated parser from FsYacc takes a lexer function (LexBuffer<char> -> token) and returns the result type.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "4eaeb639-814e-4b7a-b8cf-a63d1b091a9a", "tool_call": {"id": "4eaeb639-814e-4b7a-b8cf-a63d1b091a9a", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"The current approach has a problem: I'm trying to manually evaluate tokens in Program.fs, but the real power of FsLexYacc is that the parser (Parser.fsy) generates a Parser.parse function that takes a lexer and returns the result. The parser actions in the .fsy file can directly compute the result.\\n\\nThe proper approach:\\n1. Lexer.fsl generates Lexer.fs - a module with a `tokenize` function\\n2. Parser.fsy generates Parser.fs - a module with a `parse` function that takes a lexer and returns the result\\n3. Program.fs just calls Parser.parse with the lexer\\n\\nThe parser actions in Parser.fsy should directly compute the integer result. The grammar I wrote already does this - each production returns an int.\\n\\nLet me fix the approach:\\n- The .fsy file generates Parser.fs with a `parse` function\\n- The .fsl file generates Lexer.fs with a `tokenize` function  \\n- Program.fs calls Parser.parse with the lexer buffer\\n\\nI need to update Program.fs to use the generated parser properly. The generated parser from FsYacc takes a lexer function (LexBuffer<char> -> token) and returns the result type.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-b5168ccd-77e8-4fe8-8bc2-5e9a5c5abea7", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"The current approach has a problem: I'm trying to manually evaluate tokens in Program.fs, but the real power of FsLexYacc is that the parser (Parser.fsy) generates a Parser.parse function that takes a lexer and returns the result. The parser actions in the .fsy file can directly compute the result.\\n\\nThe proper approach:\\n1. Lexer.fsl generates Lexer.fs - a module with a `tokenize` function\\n2. Parser.fsy generates Parser.fs - a module with a `parse` function that takes a lexer and returns the result\\n3. Program.fs just calls Parser.parse with the lexer\\n\\nThe parser actions in Parser.fsy should directly compute the integer result. The grammar I wrote already does this - each production returns an int.\\n\\nLet me fix the approach:\\n- The .fsy file generates Parser.fs with a `parse` function\\n- The .fsl file generates Lexer.fs with a `tokenize` function  \\n- Program.fs calls Parser.parse with the lexer buffer\\n\\nI need to update Program.fs to use the generated parser properly. The generated parser from FsYacc takes a lexer function (LexBuffer<char> -> token) and returns the result type.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
