{"id": "d3aac036-e347-474a-8ed2-8b15bba1d370", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T10:48:57.026716", "source": "agent", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The lexer file has an issue. Let me check it:\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat -n Lexer.fsl", "is_input": false, "timeout": null, "reset": false, "kind": "TerminalAction"}, "tool_name": "terminal", "tool_call_id": "0d5d592c-e25c-408b-b052-9778e38351a3", "tool_call": {"id": "0d5d592c-e25c-408b-b052-9778e38351a3", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "terminal", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat -n Lexer.fsl\", \"summary\": \"Check Lexer.fsl content with line numbers\", \"security_risk\": \"LOW\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-5b4fbc4d-4860-40bd-ac0b-6541b4482c11", "security_risk": "LOW", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Check Lexer.fsl content with line numbers", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
{"id": "d3cf143d-b371-407a-9fb4-3c610644fe96", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T10:48:57.232039", "source": "environment", "tool_name": "terminal", "tool_call_id": "0d5d592c-e25c-408b-b052-9778e38351a3", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "1\t{\n     2\tmodule Lexer\n     3\n     4\topen System\n     5\topen Parser\n     6\t}\n     7\n     8\tlet whitespace = [' ' '\\t' '\\r' '\\n']\n     9\tlet digit = ['0'-'9']\n    10\n    11\trule tokenize = parse\n    12\t  | whitespace+    { tokenize lexbuf }\n    13\t  | digit+         { int_of_string (lexeme lexbuf) |> Tok.INTEGER }\n    14\t  | '+'            { Tok.PLUS }\n    15\t  | '-'            { Tok.MINUS }\n    16\t  | '*'            { Tok.TIMES }\n    17\t  | '/'            { Tok.DIVIDE }\n    18\t  | '('            { Tok.LPAREN }\n    19\t  | ')'            { Tok.RPAREN }\n    20\t  | eof            { Tok.EOF }\n    21\t  | _              { invalid_arg (sprintf \"Unexpected character: %s\" (lexeme_string lexbuf)) }\n    22\t}"}], "is_error": false, "command": "cat -n Lexer.fsl", "exit_code": 0, "timeout": false, "metadata": {"exit_code": 0, "pid": -1, "username": "ohama", "hostname": "ohama", "working_dir": "/Users/ohama/projs/OpenHandsTests/oh-workdir-planning/arm-b/fsharp-r2", "py_interpreter_path": "", "prefix": "", "suffix": "\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]"}, "full_output_save_dir": "/Users/ohama/.openhands/conversations/a997c64650834106a096375707160598/observations", "kind": "TerminalObservation"}, "action_id": "d3aac036-e347-474a-8ed2-8b15bba1d370", "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "cc1ada3a-8231-45e0-9473-04b7bc1ef7a2", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T10:49:06.049952", "source": "agent", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The issue is that the lexer header uses `open Parser` which doesn't exist yet (it's generated from the .fsy). Also, the token type `Tok.INTEGER` needs to be defined. In FsLexYacc, the tokens are defined in the parser's generated module. Let me fix the lexer to use the correct token module name. The parser generates a module with the same name as the file (Parser), and the tokens are in `Parser.token` or similar. Let me check the generated parser structure:\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "The issue is that the lexer references `Tok.INTEGER`, `Tok.PLUS`, etc., but these tokens are defined in the parser's generated code. In FsLexYacc:\n\n1. The parser (.fsy) generates a module named after the file (Parser) with a `token` type and token constructors\n2. The lexer (.fsl) needs to return values of that `token` type\n\nThe standard approach is:\n- The lexer header should `open Parser` to get access to the token type\n- The lexer should return `Parser.token` values (e.g., `Parser.INTEGER`, `Parser.PLUS`, etc.)\n\nBut there's a chicken-and-egg problem: the parser hasn't been generated yet when the lexer is being processed. The solution is that the .fsy file generates both the parser module AND the token type. The lexer just needs to reference the token type.\n\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc docs more carefully:\n- The parser generates a module with the filename as the module name\n- The token type is `Parser.token` \n- Token constructors are `Parser.INTEGER`, `Parser.PLUS`, etc.\n\nSo the lexer should use `Parser.INTEGER n` instead of `Tok.INTEGER n`.\n\nLet me also check: the lexer header says `open Parser` but Parser.fs doesn't exist yet. The build order should handle this - FsYacc runs before FsLex (or they run in parallel since they're independent). Actually, looking at the targets file, both targets run before CoreCompile, so the generated files should be available.\n\nWait, actually looking more carefully at the targets:\n- CallFsLex outputs to Lexer.fs\n- CallFsYacc outputs to Parser.fs\n\nBoth run before CoreCompile. So when the F# compiler compiles, both Lexer.fs and Parser.fs exist. The `open Parser` in the lexer header should work.\n\nThe real issue is the token naming. Let me fix the lexer to use `Parser.INTEGER` etc.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "96d753a4-a356-4a56-90f5-cfaa97445fba", "tool_call": {"id": "96d753a4-a356-4a56-90f5-cfaa97445fba", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"The issue is that the lexer references `Tok.INTEGER`, `Tok.PLUS`, etc., but these tokens are defined in the parser's generated code. In FsLexYacc:\\n\\n1. The parser (.fsy) generates a module named after the file (Parser) with a `token` type and token constructors\\n2. The lexer (.fsl) needs to return values of that `token` type\\n\\nThe standard approach is:\\n- The lexer header should `open Parser` to get access to the token type\\n- The lexer should return `Parser.token` values (e.g., `Parser.INTEGER`, `Parser.PLUS`, etc.)\\n\\nBut there's a chicken-and-egg problem: the parser hasn't been generated yet when the lexer is being processed. The solution is that the .fsy file generates both the parser module AND the token type. The lexer just needs to reference the token type.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc docs more carefully:\\n- The parser generates a module with the filename as the module name\\n- The token type is `Parser.token` \\n- Token constructors are `Parser.INTEGER`, `Parser.PLUS`, etc.\\n\\nSo the lexer should use `Parser.INTEGER n` instead of `Tok.INTEGER n`.\\n\\nLet me also check: the lexer header says `open Parser` but Parser.fs doesn't exist yet. The build order should handle this - FsYacc runs before FsLex (or they run in parallel since they're independent). Actually, looking at the targets file, both targets run before CoreCompile, so the generated files should be available.\\n\\nWait, actually looking more carefully at the targets:\\n- CallFsLex outputs to Lexer.fs\\n- CallFsYacc outputs to Parser.fs\\n\\nBoth run before CoreCompile. So when the F# compiler compiles, both Lexer.fs and Parser.fs exist. The `open Parser` in the lexer header should work.\\n\\nThe real issue is the token naming. Let me fix the lexer to use `Parser.INTEGER` etc.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-120c8ced-f643-4d5c-a135-f4dc029a4347", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"The issue is that the lexer references `Tok.INTEGER`, `Tok.PLUS`, etc., but these tokens are defined in the parser's generated code. In FsLexYacc:\\n\\n1. The parser (.fsy) generates a module named after the file (Parser) with a `token` type and token constructors\\n2. The lexer (.fsl) needs to return values of that `token` type\\n\\nThe standard approach is:\\n- The lexer header should `open Parser` to get access to the token type\\n- The lexer should return `Parser.token` values (e.g., `Parser.INTEGER`, `Parser.PLUS`, etc.)\\n\\nBut there's a chicken-and-egg problem: the parser hasn't been generated yet when the lexer is being processed. The solution is that the .fsy file generates both the parser module AND the token type. The lexer just needs to reference the token type.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc docs more carefully:\\n- The parser generates a module with the filename as the module name\\n- The token type is `Parser.token` \\n- Token constructors are `Parser.INTEGER`, `Parser.PLUS`, etc.\\n\\nSo the lexer should use `Parser.INTEGER n` instead of `Tok.INTEGER n`.\\n\\nLet me also check: the lexer header says `open Parser` but Parser.fs doesn't exist yet. The build order should handle this - FsYacc runs before FsLex (or they run in parallel since they're independent). Actually, looking at the targets file, both targets run before CoreCompile, so the generated files should be available.\\n\\nWait, actually looking more carefully at the targets:\\n- CallFsLex outputs to Lexer.fs\\n- CallFsYacc outputs to Parser.fs\\n\\nBoth run before CoreCompile. So when the F# compiler compiles, both Lexer.fs and Parser.fs exist. The `open Parser` in the lexer header should work.\\n\\nThe real issue is the token naming. Let me fix the lexer to use `Parser.INTEGER` etc.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
