{"id": "45175cff-bfad-44cc-ac85-547b35e0e57b", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T10:51:20.180629", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "Looking at the FsLex parser, I can see the token types. The key tokens are:\n- `BAR` = `|`\n- `DASH` = `-`\n- `LBRACK` = `[`\n- `RBRACK` = `]`\n- `LPAREN` = `(`\n- `RPAREN` = `)`\n- `CODE` = the F# code block in braces\n- `IDENT` = identifiers\n- `PARSE` = `parse` keyword\n- `RULE` = `rule` keyword\n- `LET` = `let` keyword\n\nThe error \"Unexpected character '}'\" at line 22, column 0 means the lexer parser doesn't expect `}` there. \n\nLooking at the FsLex format, the structure is:\n```\n{\n  header code\n}\n\nlet macros = ...\n\nrule name = parse\n  | pattern { action }\n  | pattern { action }\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThe closing `}` of the rule block must be on a line by itself. But the issue is that the `}` at the end of the F# action code `{ failwith (...) }` is being consumed as the lexer's closing brace.\n\nWait, I think I understand now. In FsLex, the action code is enclosed in `{ }` braces. The lexer parser needs to track brace nesting. The problem might be that the `}` inside the action code on line 21 is being seen as the end of the action, and then the `}` on line 22 is unexpected because the parser expected more clauses or the end of the file.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the FsLex format, the action code `{ ... }` is a single token (CODE). The lexer should handle nested braces. Let me check if the issue is with the `}` inside the sprintf call.\n\nWait, the sprintf call uses `sprintf \"...\"` which has parentheses, not braces. The only `}` in the action is the closing brace of the action block itself. So the structure should be:\n\n```\n| _              { failwith (sprintf \"Unexpected character: %s\" (lexeme_string lexbuf)) }\n```\n\nThis has one pair of braces: `{ ... }`. The `}` at the end closes the action. Then the next `}` on the next line should close the rule.\n\nHmm, but the error says line 22, column 0. Let me count the lines again. The file has 22 lines. Line 22 is the closing `}`. So the parser is seeing the `}` on line 22 as unexpected.\n\nThis means the parser thinks the rule block ended earlier. Perhaps the issue is that the `}` at the end of the action on line 21 is being treated as the end of the rule block, not the end of the action.\n\nWait, I think I see the issue now. The FsLex lexer parser might not handle nested braces in action code properly. Let me try using a simpler action that doesn't have any braces:\n\nInstead of:\n```\n| _              { failwith (sprintf \"Unexpected character: %s\" (lexeme_string lexbuf)) }\n```\n\nUse:\n```\n| _              { raise (System.ArgumentException \"Unexpected character\") }\n```\n\nBut I already tried that and it still failed. Let me look at the actual FsLex lexer file (fslexlex.fs) more carefully to see how it handles the CODE token.\n\nActually, looking at the fslexlex.fs file, I see it starts with:\n```\n# 1 \"fslexlex.fsl\"\n```\n\nThis means fslexlex.fs was generated from fslexlex.fsl. Let me find the original .fsl file.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "48817a1b-b005-4478-ad63-b33db7055de3", "tool_call": {"id": "48817a1b-b005-4478-ad63-b33db7055de3", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"Looking at the FsLex parser, I can see the token types. The key tokens are:\\n- `BAR` = `|`\\n- `DASH` = `-`\\n- `LBRACK` = `[`\\n- `RBRACK` = `]`\\n- `LPAREN` = `(`\\n- `RPAREN` = `)`\\n- `CODE` = the F# code block in braces\\n- `IDENT` = identifiers\\n- `PARSE` = `parse` keyword\\n- `RULE` = `rule` keyword\\n- `LET` = `let` keyword\\n\\nThe error \\\"Unexpected character '}'\\\" at line 22, column 0 means the lexer parser doesn't expect `}` there. \\n\\nLooking at the FsLex format, the structure is:\\n```\\n{\\n  header code\\n}\\n\\nlet macros = ...\\n\\nrule nam